SCIENTIFIC CONCLUSION FROM CURRENT NORMALIZED RESULTS
------------------------------------------------------
Directly demonstrated: Ring identity is the dominant predictive variable in every normalized descriptor examined. Basis Set has consistently smaller grouped SHAP and grouped permutation importance than Ring. Adding Basis Set provides little or no grouped-CV improvement for |Q2|_(ring atoms), |Q2|(0), and |Q2|(-1); the |Q2|(1) improvement is modest but occurs while grouped predictive performance remains poor.

Consistent with the proposed hypothesis: |Q2|_(ring atoms) and |Q2|(0) appear comparatively insensitive to basis-set identity in the sense of small grouped permutation importance and small or negative ablation gains. The evidence is stronger for |Q2|(0) than for |Q2|_(ring atoms) in SHAP magnitude, although |Q2|_(ring atoms) remains dominated by Ring.

Not supported by the current normalized ML results: the broad expectation that both |Q2|(1) and |Q2|(-1) should show substantially greater basis-set sensitivity. |Q2|(1) has a modest positive ablation Delta R2, but |Q2|(-1) does not. Reverse classification also does not show elevated basis-set predictability for either +/-1 descriptor.

Exploratory observation: Q2(0)zz shows the highest reverse classification accuracy, despite low basis-set SHAP and permutation importance relative to Ring. This discrepancy illustrates why classification, SHAP, and ablation should be treated as complementary rather than interchangeable evidence.

Recommended manuscript wording:
"For the normalized data, grouped repeated cross-validation, grouped SHAP, and joint-variable permutation importance consistently indicated that System/Ring identity dominated prediction and that Basis Set contributed comparatively little. Ablation results showed negligible additional predictive information from Basis Set for |Q2|_(ring atoms) and |Q2|(0). The evidence for enhanced basis-set sensitivity of the +/-1 descriptors was not uniform: |Q2|(1) showed a modest ablation gain, whereas |Q2|(-1) did not, and both reverse-classification results remained near the four-class random baseline. These results provide a complementary data-driven indication, rather than proof, of basis-set dependence or independence."
